# ==============================================================================
# The aggregate ceiling for the CI lanes
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Second slice on this host, and it exists for the reason app-agents.slice states
# first: per-unit limits are not a mechanism for a fleet whose unit count is a
# variable. A lane is a `podman run --rm` under a transient scope, and a lane
# peaks exactly when the other one starts. So the ceiling goes one level up and
# the kernel enforces the sum.
#
# THE `app-` PREFIX IS LOAD-BEARING, not decoration. systemd derives the
# hierarchy from the dashes in the name, so this nests under app.slice, where
# every quadlet already lives. A top-level `ci.slice` would put these cgroups
# outside the path bin/collect-metrics.py joins on - the failure app-agents.slice
# documents at 32 of 43 series, with nothing but a counter to say so.
#
# SIZED AGAINST THE MEASUREMENT, NOT AGAINST THE OTHER SLICE'S CEILING. That
# distinction is the whole of why two slices fit on a 15.8 GB host at all, and it
# was nearly got wrong: 4,608M of agents plus 6,656M of CI reads as 11.2 GB of
# reservation against a worst-observed MemAvailable of 9,715 MB, which looks
# impossible. It is not, because 4,608M is a ceiling almost never approached.
# Read out of this host's own 30-day store on 2026-08-24:
#
#   home_server_agent_slice_memory_bytes   median   957 MB
#                                          p90    1,455 MB
#                                          max    3,584 MB  <- which IS MemoryHigh
#   home_server_agent_phase_in_flight      mean     0.069
#   node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes         median 11,279 MB
#                                          p1      9,645 MB
#
# The fleet is idle 93% of the time holding about a gigabyte, and its 30-day
# maximum is exactly its MemoryHigh - which is page cache from `uv sync` and
# `bun install` being reclaimed, the design working rather than a ceiling under
# pressure. Overcommitting ceilings is already the house style here and
# app-agents.slice says why: twenty-four quadlets declare 34.5 GiB against 15.5
# GiB of RAM and it works, because the peaks do not coincide.
#
# WHAT MAKES THAT SAFE RATHER THAN LUCKY is that bin/github-runner.sh holds the
# SECOND lane while a conduct phase is in flight, reading the same marker and the
# same heartbeat freshness rule bin/reboot-when-staged.sh uses. At 6.9% that
# costs almost nothing, and CI never drops to zero lanes.
#
# THE PER-LANE LIMITS BIND BEFORE THIS DOES, deliberately: two lanes at their
# scope MemoryMax of 3,584M is 7,168M against the 6,656M below, so the slice
# squeezes them rather than the kernel choosing a victim by badness across the
# whole subtree. That is the sizing docs/agents.md insists on.
#
# MEASURED ON 2026-08-26, WHICH IS WHAT THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO ASK FOR. It said
# every number here was a starting point and named the measurement that would
# settle them: one e2e shard in a lane, read from the scope's own cgroup. Three
# shards have now run, two of them concurrently, which is the worst case this
# file is sized against.
#
#   scope memory.peak      2,816-2,818 MB   on all three, and on api-checks too
#   scope memory.stat anon 2,607 MB worst
#   scope memory.events    high climbing to 2,197; max 0; oom_kill 0
#   scope pids.peak        316 of 1,024
#   slice memory.peak      5,634 MB of 5,632M MemoryHigh
#   slice pids.peak        623 of 2,048
#   memory.pressure some   avg10 0.00-4.11%
#   shard wall clock       6m against the job's own timeout-minutes: 25
#
# NOTHING CHANGES, AND THE READING THAT SAYS SO IS `max`, NOT THE PEAK. Every
# shard pinned itself to MemoryHigh EXACTLY - 2,816M is this scope's MemoryHigh
# and three independent jobs landed on it to within 2 MB - which is the throttle
# doing precisely what it is for. `memory.events max` stayed 0 and oom_kill
# stayed 0, so the hard ceiling was never reached and no allocation was ever
# refused. app-agents.slice records the same reading being misread once already.
#
# THE MARGIN IS WHAT ABSORBED IT, and it is tighter here than next door. anon
# alone is 2,607 MB, so 93% of MemoryHigh is memory genuinely in use rather than
# reclaimable cache, and the 768M between MemoryHigh and MemoryMax is the whole
# of the headroom. app-agents has 1,042M for a workload with no browser and no
# service containers. This held across three shards; a heavier suite is what
# would cross it, and that arrives as an oom_kill rather than as a slow job.
#
# RAISING THE SCOPE ALONE WOULD MOVE THE THROTTLE, NOT ADD HEADROOM. Two lanes
# at MemoryHigh sum to 5,632M, which is this slice's MemoryHigh to the megabyte,
# so the slice pins at the same moment the lanes do. The pair moves together or
# neither does.
#
# ITS FAILURE IS NO LONGER SILENCE. bin/github-runner.sh keeps memory.peak and
# pids.peak off each transient scope in the lane's marker - the scope is
# --collect and vanishes with the container, so the driver's own poll is the
# only window there is - and `ci.lane_headroom` in bin/verify-host.sh grades
# them on the events. The next argument about these numbers starts from a
# measurement rather than from this comment.
#
# ITS FAILURE IS SILENCE, which is the whole reason ci.slice_limits reads these
# values back out of the cgroup rather than out of this file. A `Slice=` naming a
# slice with no unit file does NOT fail: systemd instantiates it with defaults,
# and every member then starts, stays healthy, stays fully observed and is
# contained by nothing at all. Verify, never assume:
#   cat /sys/fs/cgroup/user.slice/user-1000.slice/user@1000.service/app.slice/\
#       app-ci.slice/memory.max
#
# Symlinked into ~/.config/systemd/user/ - see host/systemd/README.md, whose
# setup loop already globs *.slice because app-agents.slice made it.
# ==============================================================================

[Slice]
MemoryHigh=5632M
MemoryMax=6656M

# BELOW app-agents.slice's 30 ON PURPOSE. Under contention the agent fleet is
# gating a change somebody is waiting on with a merge blocked behind it; CI is
# doing work a hosted runner would happily do a minute later. Neither should be
# able to starve the media stack, which has no weight set at all and therefore
# sits at the default 100.
CPUWeight=20
CPUQuota=400%

# THE OTHER HALF OF THAT QUOTA, and app-agents.slice paid for this lesson at 5x.
#
# CPUQuota PROTECTS THE HOST AND TELLS THE GUEST NOTHING. A container is not
# CPU-namespaced, so `nproc` and node's os.availableParallelism() read the host's
# 12 while this cgroup delivers 4. vitest, playwright, esbuild, tsc and any
# `make -j$(nproc)` then start three times the workers the quota can run, and the
# kernel enforces the difference by throttling all of them. Measured there, same
# suite, five runs: 4457 tests in 340-364s with one SPURIOUS FAILURE at nproc=12,
# against 69-71s and green at nproc=4.
#
# THIS VALUE ALONE IS STILL NOT ENOUGH HERE, and that is the difference from the
# other slice. app-agents runs one phase at a time; this runs two lanes. Both
# would read nproc=4 from this cpuset and between them start 8 workers on 4
# cores - the same defect at half the magnitude. So bin/github-runner.sh pins
# each lane scope to HALF of this, 4-5 and 6-7, and a job sees nproc=2, which is
# what it will actually get.
#
# STATED HONESTLY: 4-7 IS NOT EXCLUSIVE. No other unit on this host pins a
# cpuset, so ffmpeg, Jellyfin and the Tdarr nodes float across all twelve and
# will take these four whenever they are free. nproc=2 is therefore the truth
# about the quota and not a promise about the hardware - the mirror image of the
# sentence this comment opens with.
AllowedCPUs=4-7

IOWeight=20

# A LANE IS A CONTAINER ENGINE RUNNING A TEST SUITE, which is a different shape
# from a phase: the nested podman, a conmon per service container, three
# datastores, two Vite servers, a uvicorn under coverage and a browser all count
# here. app-agents.slice measured a pids.peak of 325 for a full gate with no
# browsers and no service containers, so 2,048 across two lanes was a guess in
# the generous direction.
#
# THE GUESS WAS RIGHT, AND IT IS NOW A MEASUREMENT. An e2e shard - the heaviest
# shape there is, with all of the above running at once - peaked at 316 of the
# scope's own TasksMax of 1,024, and two of them together took this slice to 623
# of 2,048. Both sit near 31%, which is generous rather than wasteful: the
# symptom when this binds is `fork: Resource temporarily unavailable` raised by
# something unrelated, naming neither the slice nor the limit, so the cost of
# being wrong in the tight direction is a job failure nobody can attribute.
TasksMax=2048
